    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 121641\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n940 AM PDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Isolated-to-scattered showers and thunderstorms will be possible\n  across the region today, with precipitation chances lingering \n  into Tuesday for Lincoln, Clark, and Mohave Counties. \n\n* Breezy conditions and cooler than normal temperatures will\n  continue through the week, with the first freeze of the season\n  possible for portions of the Owens Valley and southwestern Great\n  Basin.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through next weekend.\n\nLate night radar and satellite imagery show ongoing showers across \nClark, Lincoln, and Mohave Counties as an upper-level trough pushes \ninto the area. This trough will continue its slow trek east today, \nwith isolated-to-scattered showers and thunderstorms expected across \nthe region today as a result. Moisture across the region will not be \nas abundant as it was on Sunday when Rachel's remnants moved \nthrough, so widespread flash flooding is not expected to pose as \ngreat of a threat today. However, isolated instances of flash \nflooding will be possible with stronger storms that develop. In \naddition to isolated instances of flash flooding, lightning, gusty \nthunderstorm winds, and localized moderate-to-heavy rain will be \npossible with storms today. The upper-level trough will continue to \ndig through the region on Tuesday, with around a 20 to 50 percent \nchance for isolated showers and thunderstorms across the Arizona \nStrip, northeastern Clark County, and eastern Lincoln County on \nTuesday afternoon. By Wednesday, precipitation chances will have \ndisappeared from the area as the trough shifts off to our east. \n\nIn addition to showers and thunderstorms, this trough will also \nbreezy conditions to southern Nevada, northwestern Arizona, and \nsoutheastern California. Breezy southerly winds are expected today \nwith peak gusts in the 25 to 40 mph range this afternoon. Winds will \nshift to the northwest on Tuesday as a cold front swings through the \narea, with breezier northerly winds lingering through the work week. \nAs the cold front moves through, some of the cities/towns in the \nnorthwestern portion of our forecast area will have a shot at their \nfirst freeze of the season. Currently, the NBM boasts an 80 to 95% \nchance of freezing temperatures on Tuesday morning for the Bishop \nand Dyer areas, where a Freeze Watch remains in effect. Looking at \nthe rest of the region, temperatures on Tuesday will run around 15 \nto 20 degrees cooler than normal. Beyond Tuesday, temperatures will \ngradually rebound during the second half of the week with \ntemperatures approaching normal over the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package... \nScattered showers will continue to be possible across the Valley \nthrough the afternoon, with brief showers bringing the CIGS down to \nbetween 6-7kft AGL if they move over the airfield. Otherwise, \nsouthwest winds with gusts to 25 knots will continue through the \nafternoon. A turn to the northwest is expected Tuesday morning, but \nconfidence in the timing of the shift is low at this time. CIGs \naround 10kft AGL will be seen into this evening, followed by \nimproving skies overnight.  \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Scattered showers and \nisolated thunderstorms will continue to be possible across the \neastern half of the region this afternoon, potentially resulting in \nimpacts at all regional TAF sites except KDAG. Winds will remain \nlight into mid-morning before gusty south to southwest winds \ndevelop. The exception will be KBIH, where a turn to the north is \nexpected after 21Z. CIGs between 8kft and 10kft AGL are expected at \nmost regional TAF sites into this evening, followed by improving \nskies overnight at most locations. Near shower and thunderstorm \nactivity today, CIGs could fall to 7kft to 8kft AGL and localized \nreductions in visibility may be seen. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Stessman\nAVIATION...Planz/CO\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
